WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.220
Burned amino acids split by atom: nitrogen is caged as urea, and carbon enters

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.220
through seven doors.

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.300
Now picture urine that turns black.

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.220
In 1902, Archibald Garrod argued that this disease, alkaptonuria, is inherited.

5
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.700
How can a color point to one broken step?

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.950
Each carbon skeleton reaches its door through a line of enzymes.

7
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.990
Every enzyme does one step.

8
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.830
Some steps need a vitamin helper, a cofactor.

9
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:40.230
The cofactor is a fingerprint: it tells you what kind of step it is.

10
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:43.343
Take the succinyl-CoA door.

11
00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:50.303
Valine, isoleucine, methionine and threonine are broken down to propionyl-CoA.

12
00:00:51.543 --> 00:00:55.843
A biotin enzyme carboxylates it to methylmalonyl-CoA.

13
00:00:57.003 --> 00:01:01.283
Then a B12 enzyme rearranges that into succinyl-CoA.

14
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:04.143
Biotin means carboxylation.

15
00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:06.923
B12 means rearrangement.

16
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.460
Now jam one station.

17
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.900
Its enzyme is defective, but molecules keep arriving from upstream.

18
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.200
Which will rise: the molecule the station takes in, or the one it makes?

19
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:27.557
The one it takes in.

20
00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:31.357
With nowhere to go, it piles up behind the jam.

21
00:01:32.597 --> 00:01:34.777
It can spill out into the blood and the urine.

22
00:01:35.957 --> 00:01:39.537
A broken step announces itself by what piles up behind it.

23
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.260
In PKU, phenylalanine hydroxylase is defective.

24
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.080
Phenylalanine, the molecule it takes in, rises in the blood.

25
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.580
In many countries, newborn screening looks for exactly that, in a dried blood spot

26
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:56.980
taken days after birth.

27
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:03.583
In maple syrup urine disease, a branched-chain keto-acid dehydrogenase is defective.

28
00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:09.263
Branched-chain amino acids and their keto acids build up behind it.

29
00:02:10.323 --> 00:02:14.743
The urine smells of maple syrup, and the excess leucine is neurotoxic.

30
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:18.193
The nitrogen road obeys the same rule.

31
00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:22.273
When the urea cycle fails, ammonia piles up.

32
00:02:23.713 --> 00:02:27.033
Astrocytes swell as they trap it in glutamine, and the brain swells.

33
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:29.853
Back to Garrod's black urine.

34
00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:34.913
In alkaptonuria, a molecule called homogentisate piles up.

35
00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:39.413
It oxidizes to a black pigment, so the urine darkens.

36
00:02:40.813 --> 00:02:45.373
Alkaptonuria became the first recognized inborn error of metabolism.

37
00:02:46.453 --> 00:02:49.553
Each such error is rare, but as a group they are not.

38
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:53.707
So read a metabolic disease like a jammed line.

39
00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:58.507
Find what has piled up, and the broken step sits just past it.

40
00:02:59.487 --> 00:03:01.707
Then ask which cofactor that step needs.
